  "image_name": "882b0ee4e41054914d6220b17ac01c83.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how spin-polarized electrons passing through a magnetic tunnel junction affect a “free” magnetic layer above a fixed “reference” layer. In all three panels, electrons travel upward from the blue reference magnet, through a gray tunnel barrier, into the red free magnet. The reference layer sets the incoming electron spins mostly upward, creating a net spin current.\n\nThe main idea is that the spin direction of the current is compared with the magnetization of the free layer, labeled \\(M_F\\), which is tilted relative to the reference magnetization \\(M_R\\). In the simplest view, the incoming spins are not aligned with the free layer, so their angular momentum can be transferred to it. The middle and right panels make that mechanism more explicit by showing that, within a few monolayers of the free magnet, the electron spins appear to precess and reorient toward the local magnetization. As they do so, torque is exerted on the free layer’s magnetic moment.\n\nOverall, the sequence conveys spin-transfer torque: a polarized current generated by the reference magnet enters the free magnet, rapidly adapts to its magnetization, and in the process can push or rotate the free layer’s magnetic orientation.",
